{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 13, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3-next-80b-a3b-instruct", "content": "*highlighted section part 1*  \nRaymond III was the Count of Tripoli from 1152 until his death in 1187. He was born in 1140 and was the son of Raymond II and Hodierna of Jerusalem. Raymond inherited the county at a young age and faced immediate challenges due to his minority. His early rule was marked by regencies and political instability as powerful nobles and neighboring rulers vied for influence over the young count. The County of Tripoli was one of the four major Crusader states and held strategic importance along the Levantine coast. Raymond III was known for his cautious nature and diplomatic skills which helped him navigate the turbulent politics of the region.\n\n*highlighted section part 2*  \nRaymond III played a critical role in the politics of the Kingdom of Jerusalem. He was married to a daughter of King Baldwin III which strengthened his ties to the royal family. After the death of King Amalric in 1174 Raymond became regent for the young Baldwin IV who suffered from leprosy. Raymond served as regent during the early years of Baldwin IVs reign and was instrumental in managing the kingdom’s defense against Saladin. His leadership was often challenged by other nobles such as Raynald of Châtillon and the Knights Templar. Raymond’s reluctance to engage in aggressive military campaigns contrasted sharply with the more militant factions at court. This division weakened the Crusader states and made them vulnerable to external threats.\n\n*highlighted section part 3*  \nRaymond III was eventually removed from the regency in 1176 due to political maneuvering by his rivals. He spent the next decade in relative isolation but returned to power after Baldwin IVs death in 1185. He was appointed guardian of the new king Baldwin V. Raymond led the Crusader army at the Battle of Hattin in 1187 where the Crusaders suffered a crushing defeat. He survived the battle but the loss led to the fall of Jerusalem and the collapse of much of the Crusader territory. Raymond III died later that year in 1187. His death marked the end of a long and complex reign defined by diplomacy caution and the struggle to preserve Crusader holdings amid internal discord and rising Muslim power. His legacy remains a subject of debate among historians who view him as both a prudent statesman and a hesitant leader in a time that demanded bold action.", "logprobs": [{"token": "*", "logprob": -5.1973900554003194e-05}, {"token": "highlight", "logprob": -0.0031869832891970873}, {"token": "ed", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " section", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " part", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " ", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "*", "logprob": -3.2782016205601394e-05}, {"token": "  \n", "logprob": -0.008615112863481045}, {"token": "Ray", "logprob": -5.960462772236497e-07}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": -4.768370445162873e-07}, {"token": " III", "logprob": -8.34461570775602e-06}, {"token": " was", "logprob": -0.0028299542609602213}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -0.005314982496201992}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -0.08057541400194168}, {"token": " of", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " Trip", "logprob": -9.417489309271332e-06}, {"token": "oli", "logprob": -3.576278118089249e-07}, {"token": " from", "logprob": -0.0008200620068237185}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -0.0001754606782924384}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -4.768370445162873e-07}, {"token": "5", "logprob": -1.9430925021879375e-05}, {"token": "2", "logprob": -1.5497195136049413e-06}, {"token": " until", "logprob": -0.3868868052959442}, {"token": " his", "logprob": -0.0020560333505272865}, {"token": " death", "logprob": -2.861018856492592e-06}, {"token": " in", "logprob": -1.3470558769768104e-05}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -2.50339189733495e-06}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "8", "logprob": 